WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.660
There is plenty of opportunity
when we speak a foreign language

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.520
to come across as silly.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today I want to talk about cultural

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:16.140
sensitivities and the specific
cultural aspects of a language and why

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.300
we should ignore them when we start
into a new language and when it's

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.150
appropriate to be more focused on this.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.770
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.330
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.280
Um, if you're following me on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.620
So the reason I bring this up is that, uh,
I was on Twitter and I had done a video

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.510
earlier where I said that when I start a
new language, I'm really not interested

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:52.260
in the sort of detail, you know, names
of relatives because many languages have

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.870
more specific terms for relatives, you
know, nd uncle is not just an uncle,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.130
it's either the mother, his brother, or
the father's brother or cousin could be

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.680
any number of combinations of relatives.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.220
And so an older sister, younger
sister, older brother, younger brother,

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:12.480
all of these things are, in some
languages there's a lot of detail

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:13.650
there that we don't have in English.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.800
And these are things that we eventually
need to know if we're going to

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.270
be operating comfortably in the
language, but it's not a good idea in

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:26.470
my experience to be confronted with
these things at an early stage in

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:32.440
the language, uh, the same goes with
sort of polite language or different

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:38.440
level of, uh, levels of politeness or
introducing, you know, special festivals.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.089
I remember in Japanese, there
was early on, there was a

00:01:42.089 --> 00:01:44.640
lesson on irises and blue flags.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.720
It's some festival in Japan that
I still don't know anything about.

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:54.539
There's a tendency on the part of
people who write textbooks to want

00:01:54.539 --> 00:01:59.100
to focus on what is different than
call it esoteric and potentially

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.630
attractive in the new culture.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:07.919
Uh, but in my view, these things, if they
come early, Uh, in the language learning

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.650
process, they just add more difficulty.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.489
We're learning a new language,
new words, new sounds.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:19.049
My experience is the more we can
deal with things that are familiar to

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:24.030
us, familiar situations, husband and
wife, a brother and a sister without

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.990
worrying about terms for older or
younger, just people interacting with

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:31.799
each other, using common vocabulary.

00:02:31.799 --> 00:02:37.380
If we can have this more familiar
content, then it's going to be

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.640
easier to learn the language.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:44.190
And if we don't learn the term for
older sister, younger sister at the

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.580
beginner stage will eventually learn it.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.340
And the same is true with
different levels of politeness.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.040
Now, so I said something to this effect
in a video and, you know, I have been

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:02.220
having sort of extracts or excerpts of
some of my previous videos featured again.

00:03:03.075 --> 00:03:06.975
So this came up and then this generated
this flurry of activity on Twitter.

00:03:07.485 --> 00:03:11.985
Now, you know, and I can't remember
what the wording was, but somebody

00:03:11.985 --> 00:03:18.135
sort of commented, you know, uh, I'm
shocked that you would, uh, ignore

00:03:18.135 --> 00:03:21.695
these fine aspects of, uh, the culture.

00:03:22.679 --> 00:03:28.440
Um, many of the beginner words are tied
up with these aspects of the culture.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.470
And I said, well, give me some
examples and the person didn't

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.790
come back with any examples.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:36.630
Other than to say that she was
shocked that I was prepared

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.910
to be rude in the language.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:45.779
Um, you know, I think there is a tendency
when we learn a language where their

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:51.210
culture is very different or even not so
different, that we become over-sensitive

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.160
to these cultural differences.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.590
It's not easy to offend people.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.780
It's not easy to be rude.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:04.230
Uh, just because we use the
wrong word or the wrong degree

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.270
of politeness or anything else.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.240
If we are a beginner, first of all, when
we're starting in the language, in my

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.460
opinion, better off to focus on input.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.019
So you're just absorbing the
language and you may notice that

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.490
sometimes it's "vous" and sometimes
it's "tu", for example, in French.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:30.030
Polite or familiar, not familiar and
graduallywe get used to this and maybe we

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.710
find an explanation somewhere and we kind
of tuck that in the back of our minds.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:39.030
And then when we go to speak eventually
a few months later, we start speaking.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.420
We may get it wrong, but
we won't offend anyone.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:48.060
Uh, any more than if someone who is a
beginner in English speaks to us and uses

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.210
the wrong word or says something wrong.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.780
I mean, unless they're swearing at
us, we're not going to be offended.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.890
We won't necessarily
consider the person rude.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.755
We might, we might find them silly.

00:05:01.755 --> 00:05:05.385
I mean, there is plenty of
opportunity when we speak a foreign

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:07.755
language to come across as silly.

00:05:08.085 --> 00:05:09.974
That comes with the territory.

00:05:10.455 --> 00:05:16.275
You have to be willing to sound
less intelligent, uh, somewhat

00:05:16.275 --> 00:05:18.765
sillier than in your own language.

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:23.145
That is something we have to accept if
we're speaking a foreign language, but

00:05:23.145 --> 00:05:28.859
at the early stages, I think everything
should be done to make it as easy as

00:05:28.859 --> 00:05:32.219
possible to get the language in you.

00:05:32.219 --> 00:05:37.080
So familiar situations, but here are these
different words and here is how they say

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.489
good morning, or here is how they say I'm
going to work today or whatever it is.

00:05:41.489 --> 00:05:45.690
Very standard type, uh,
words and situations.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.780
That's why our mini stories at LingQ are
largely call them culturally neutral.

00:05:52.299 --> 00:05:54.359
They're not completely culturally neutral.

00:05:54.359 --> 00:05:55.249
That's not possible.

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:59.070
They were written by someone
who, actually a Canadian woman

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.510
who teaches English in Japan.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:04.770
So she's going to reflect that to some
extent, but in many cultures of the

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.540
world, we get up, we make breakfast,
people go to work, uh, people might

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.050
be cleaning their apartment or
their house or any of the different

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.880
situations that we have there are
relatively, relatively cultural neutral.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.920
They don't go into very culturally
specific things in our mini stories.

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:25.950
Those are our existing 60 mini stories.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.479
I think it would be great to have
some culturally specific mini

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:35.280
stories so that the principle of the
many stories, the repetition, the

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.120
high-frequency verbs, we contain...

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.150
we sort of maintain that, but we introduce
things that are specific to Persian

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.969
culture, which would be fine for me
because I'm learning Persian or Arabic

00:06:44.969 --> 00:06:53.040
culture or uh, or, or France or China or
Russia or Japan, so we can have those.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.580
Uh, I think those are better later.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:02.010
Now, bare in mind, I'm talking about
my personal perspective on this, of

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:05.370
course, I eventually wanted to get
into the cultural, uh, culturally

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:09.600
specific things, and I want to choose
those things that I find interesting.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:15.600
And it may be that, uh, you know,
Japanese cooking is what I'm interested

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.760
in more so than say Japanese dance, for
example, or I'm not interested in anime.

00:07:21.034 --> 00:07:24.335
We can choose those things that
we're interested in, and that will

00:07:24.335 --> 00:07:26.585
inspire us further in our study.

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:31.625
But if at an early stage, we don't get
the degree of politeness correct in

00:07:31.625 --> 00:07:36.245
Korean or Japanese, that doesn't mean we
won't be able to get it right later on.

00:07:36.425 --> 00:07:39.395
This is another thing, cause there's a
number of exchanges here on Twitter and

00:07:39.664 --> 00:07:42.695
people implying well, you know, if you
don't get it right at the beginning,

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.805
you'll forever be speaking impolitely.

00:07:45.065 --> 00:07:45.905
That's not true.

00:07:46.205 --> 00:07:49.895
Uh, there are any number of things
that we get wrong at the early stage.

00:07:50.625 --> 00:07:54.655
From the tense to the
person, to the gender to...

00:07:54.825 --> 00:07:58.485
and that doesn't mean that at some
stage, because we've been listening

00:07:58.485 --> 00:08:02.235
and reading so much and becoming more
and more familiar with the language at

00:08:02.235 --> 00:08:05.895
some stage, we'll get better and we'll
get better with all of these aspects.

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:11.865
So I think the, this idea that you have
to get, and it's a bit like, you know,

00:08:11.925 --> 00:08:14.835
a lot of the things that are typically
introduced early on in, in these

00:08:14.835 --> 00:08:18.855
beginner books, including colors, for
example, I have no capacity for absorbing

00:08:18.855 --> 00:08:23.655
seven colours, uh, in one lesson,
uh, or all the numbers in one lesson.

00:08:24.135 --> 00:08:29.505
Uh, it's just part and parcel of all the
things that will gradually be, be absorbed

00:08:29.505 --> 00:08:35.895
by the brain in due course, and ideally
absorbed within content that we can

00:08:35.895 --> 00:08:38.564
cope with, that is relatively familiar.

00:08:39.794 --> 00:08:43.995
And, you know, words that repeat
often and gradually we get better

00:08:43.995 --> 00:08:47.985
and as we build up, we pursue
areas of interest or we get better.

00:08:48.195 --> 00:08:51.195
And we realize, actually, if you're
talking about levels of politeness

00:08:51.195 --> 00:08:55.785
in Japanese and Chinese, we realize
as we understand and hear better,

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:56.535
what other people are saying.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.410
And we understand better in what
situations, which words are used,

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.150
which levels of politeness are used.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.120
We then get better at
doing that ourselves.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:11.010
Uh, and that's part of the reason why
I'm very much against, uh, teaching slang

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:18.210
because you, you also have to have a
good sense of where slang is appropriate.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.814
Not to mention swear words which
can be used at some point but

00:09:22.814 --> 00:09:25.905
are best avoided until we have a
good sense of when to use them.

00:09:26.025 --> 00:09:30.165
Similarly, the levels of politeness,
we will eventually develop

00:09:30.165 --> 00:09:31.844
a feel for when to use them.

00:09:32.535 --> 00:09:38.295
And the idea that somehow people in other
cultures are easily offended if we get

00:09:38.295 --> 00:09:44.880
things wrong, even though we know that we
ourselves wouldn't be by someone getting

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.100
similar things wrong, why would they be?

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.600
I think there's sort of an over
sensitivity to what we perceive

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.170
to be more, you know, exotic
cultures that we have to step

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:57.390
very carefully, walk on eggshells.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:58.620
I don't believe that.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.390
I think people are similar everywhere.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.449
They encourage you to learn the language.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.550
Not everyone, not everyone.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.560
There will be people
who might be offended.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.040
There are people who have no
patience sort of to deal with you.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.829
If you're learning their language
and you butcher it, there's always

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:16.560
those people, but the majority
of people aren't that way.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.650
So I just thought I would comment on that.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.630
So when you get started in the
language, I say, go easy on the culture.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:25.335
You'll get to it eventually.

00:10:25.335 --> 00:10:29.055
And you'll find those things that
interest you and, and presumably it's,

00:10:29.065 --> 00:10:33.885
uh, it's, uh, a general interest in
the culture that has encouraged you

00:10:33.885 --> 00:10:38.385
sort of to learn the language, but
it'll all come and do course you don't

00:10:38.385 --> 00:10:40.445
have to get it in the big enterprise.

00:10:40.805 --> 00:10:45.329
So, that would be something that I've
been engaging with people on Twitter.

00:10:45.329 --> 00:10:48.660
And of course, Twitter is a very
special kind of medium people

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:52.380
tend to just say things without
really having thought them through.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.839
Uh, but it's, it's fun.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:55.199
It's...

00:10:55.199 --> 00:10:59.189
you get a sense of what some people are
thinking, how representative they are.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.319
It's hard to gauge because if
you're in a particular silo, you'll

00:11:04.319 --> 00:11:07.920
get a lot of people commenting,
reflecting a similar point of view

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.839
that may not be very representative.

00:11:10.454 --> 00:11:15.435
So, um, yeah, with that, the idea of
culture, I've always been interested

00:11:15.435 --> 00:11:16.875
in culture, but not upfront.

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:20.535
And I leave you with a couple of videos
that talk about getting started in

00:11:20.535 --> 00:11:22.485
a language and the role of culture.

00:11:23.115 --> 00:11:23.865
Thank you for listening.

00:11:24.194 --> 00:11:24.525
Bye for now.

